Every permutation $\sigma$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ arises exactly once by inserting $n$ into a permutation $\sigma'$ of $\{1,\dots,n-1\}$ (delete $n$ to recover $\sigma'$). Let $\sigma'$ have $j$ ascents and $n-2-j$ descents. Inserting $n$:

- at the very beginning: no new ascent ($n>$ next), so $j$ ascents;
- at the very end: the last entry is $<n$, a new ascent: $j+1$;
- between a pair forming an ascent $a<b$: it becomes $a<n>b$, still one ascent: $j$;
- between a pair forming a descent $a>b$: it becomes $a<n>b$, which creates an ascent: $j+1$.

So from $\sigma'$ with $k$ ascents we get $\sigma$ with $k$ ascents in $1+k$ ways (beginning or at one of the $k$ ascents). From $\sigma'$ with $k-1$ ascents, which has $(n-2)-(k-1)=n-k-1$ descents, we get $k$ ascents in $1+(n-k-1)=n-k$ ways (end or at one of the descents). Adding: $\left\langle {n \atop k}\right\rangle=(k+1)\left\langle {n-1 \atop k}\right\rangle+(n-k)\left\langle {n-1 \atop k-1}\right\rangle$. ∎
